{0 Deploy}

This page puts the Silica site ([site/]) in production: as a container
(Docker, Fly.io), or as static files (Cloudflare Pages, Vercel). Another app
built on the [tiny_httpd] adapter does the same; the commands name the site.

{1:build The production build}

[dune build] makes the production build: the client is minified, split into
an entry, shared chunks and the chunks of lazy routes (see
{!page-router.lazy}), and each file gets a [.br] (brotli 11) and a [.gz] copy
next to it. The server sends the copy that the browser accepts for hashed
files, with [vary: accept-encoding]. The esbuild rule of the site:

From [site/client/dune]:
{@dune skip[
    --minify=%{env:SILICA_MINIFY=true}
    --splitting
    --outdir=dist
    --entry-names=[name]-[hash]
    --chunk-names=[name]-[hash]
]}

[SILICA_MINIFY=false dune build] gives the debug build, not minified, with the
same file names. [dune test] checks the size of the production build against
its budgets (see {!page-performance}); it skips that check for a debug build.

The server does not compress HTML pages: the switch has no zlib binding, and
pages are [private, no-store], so they cannot be compressed ahead. Put a proxy
that compresses [text/html] in front (Fly.io and Cloudflare do; for Caddy,
[encode zstd gzip]; for nginx, [gzip on]). It matters for large pages: the
mini Linear list with 1000 issues is 784 kB, 45 kB with gzip.

The server is one executable, [site/server/main.exe]. It needs the client
build next to it: the directory [dist] with the hashed files, and the esbuild
metafile [meta.json] that maps each logical name to its hashed file (see
{!page-server.assets}).

{1:env Environment variables}

Each variable has a flag of the same meaning ([main.exe --help]).

- [PORT]: the port, default [8080].
- [SILICA_ADDR]: the address to listen on, default [127.0.0.1]. In a
  container it must be [0.0.0.0], or the proxy cannot reach the server.
- [SILICA_STATIC]: the directory served at [/static/], default
  [_build/default/site/client/dist].
- [SILICA_METAFILE]: the esbuild metafile, default
  [_build/default/site/client/meta.json].
- [SILICA_ORIGINS]: the public origins, separated by commas, such as
  [https://silica-site.fly.dev]. Every call and every Live socket must send one
  of them in its [Origin] header. With none, only [localhost], [127.0.0.1] and
  [[::1]] work: a deployed server needs this variable.
- [SILICA_SECURE_COOKIE]: [1] adds [Secure] to the session cookie. Set it when
  the public address is HTTPS, also when a proxy ends TLS.
- [SILICA_SECRET]: the key of the CSRF tokens, at least 16 bytes (see
  {{!section-secret}The session secret}).
- [SILICA_MAX_CONNECTIONS]: the open connections at most, default [1024]. Each
  open Live socket and each idle keep-alive connection holds one, with a
  thread; a client above the limit waits until one closes.

{1:docker Docker}

The [Dockerfile] at the root of the repository has three stages. The build
stage installs the opam packages at the versions of the development switch, and
takes [node] from the Node image for the Tailwind and esbuild rules. The
[node_modules] directory of [site/client] is in the repository, so the build
needs no npm registry. The last stage has only the server and the client build,
runs as a user that is not root, and checks [/_silica/health]:

From [Dockerfile]:
{@dockerfile[
FROM debian:12-slim
RUN useradd --system --uid 10001 --no-create-home silica
COPY --from=build /home/opam/silica/_build/default/site/server/main.exe /app/site
COPY --from=build /home/opam/silica/_build/default/site/client/dist /app/dist
COPY --from=build /home/opam/silica/_build/default/site/client/meta.json /app/meta.json
ENV PORT=8080 \
    SILICA_ADDR=0.0.0.0 \
    SILICA_STATIC=/app/dist \
    SILICA_METAFILE=/app/meta.json
USER silica
]}

Build and run it, then open [http://127.0.0.1:8080/]:

{@sh[
docker build -t silica-site .
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 silica-site
]}

[docker stop] sends [SIGTERM]. The adapter then stops accepting connections,
closes each Live socket with code 1001 (going away), waits for the requests in
progress, at most 10 seconds, and exits with code 0. The browser connects a
Live socket again after a delay (see {!page-server}).

{1:fly Fly.io}

[fly.toml] runs the same image:

From [fly.toml]:
{@toml[
[env]
  PORT = "8080"
  SILICA_ADDR = "0.0.0.0"
  SILICA_ORIGINS = "https://silica-site.fly.dev"
  SILICA_SECURE_COOKIE = "1"

[http_service]
  internal_port = 8080
  force_https = true
  auto_stop_machines = "stop"
  auto_start_machines = true
  min_machines_running = 0
]}

Change [app] and [SILICA_ORIGINS] to the name of your app, then run
[fly launch --no-deploy --copy-config] once and [fly deploy --ha=false].

- The Fly proxy ends TLS and passes WebSocket upgrades through. The browser
  sends the [Origin] of the public address, so [SILICA_ORIGINS] must hold it.
- The client pings each Live socket every 30 seconds, so the socket is never
  idle for long enough for the proxy to close it.
- [kill_timeout] is 15 seconds, more than the 10 seconds the server waits for
  the requests in progress after [SIGTERM].
- With [auto_stop_machines], the machine stops when it has no connection. An
  open Live socket is a connection, so a page with Live data keeps it running.
  A stop ends the sessions: see {{!section-secret}The session secret}.
- [--ha=false] keeps one machine. The sessions and the Live state are in
  memory (see {{!section-scale}One instance}).

{1:static Static export}

A static host serves files and cannot answer a [POST]. The site still works
there, because its Remote calls only read: the export renders every page on the
server and writes the answers of the calls as files.

{@sh[
dune build @all
dune exec site/export/export.exe -- --out _build/site-static
]}

The directory has [index.html], [docs.html], [docs/<page>.html],
[components.html], [components/<name>.html], [404.html], the hashed files in
[static/], the answers in [silica-answers/] and a [_headers] file that gives the
hashed files a cache of one year. The Docker build can make the same directory
with no OCaml on the machine:
[docker build --target static --output type=local,dest=site-static .]

How it works:

- Each page is the server HTML of its route. The export replaces the
  [silica-csrf] meta element with
  [<meta name="silica-static" content="/silica-answers/">].
- For each answer that the server wrote into a page (the
  [silica-answers] script, see {!page-hydration}), the export writes the
  output at [silica-answers/<path>/<hash>.json]. The hash is FNV-1a, 32 bits,
  of the bytes of the encoded input, in 8 hex digits.
- In a page with the [silica-static] element, the browser host sends no
  [POST] for a Remote call: it reads that file with a [GET]. A call that no
  page answered gets status 404, and its machine gets [Error (Http 404)].

So the first page reads its answers from its HTML, and a link to another docs
page reads the answer file of that page, on hover as on a server.

What does not work on a static host:

- The two samples that call the server: the save sample of the landing page
  ([/api/save]) and the greeting of the {{!page-tutorial}tutorial}
  ([/api/greeting]). Their answer depends on the moment or on the input, so the
  export writes no file for them. A press shows their failed state: "Not
  saved. Try again" and "The server did not answer."
- No flash on the first paint with a stored theme: the HTML has the [system]
  theme, and the client applies the theme of the cookie after the script runs
  (see {!page-theming}).
- Live data, sessions and any call that changes data.
- The pages use paths from the root ([/static/], [/silica-answers/]), so the
  site must be at the root of its domain.

{2:cloudflare Cloudflare}

Cloudflare Pages serves the export as it is: upload the directory, or run the
export in the build command and give the directory as the output. Pages serves
[docs/router.html] at [/docs/router], [404.html] with status 404 for unknown
paths, and reads [_headers].

A Cloudflare Worker runs JavaScript and WebAssembly in V8. It cannot run the
native OCaml server. For the server there are two choices:

- Cloudflare Containers run a container image, such as the image of the
  Dockerfile, behind a Worker that sends each request to a container. A
  container sleeps when it has no requests; when it starts again, it has no
  sessions and no Live state, as after a restart.
- The Cloudflare proxy in front of a server on Fly.io: point a proxied DNS
  record at the Fly app and put the public origin in [SILICA_ORIGINS]. The
  proxy passes WebSockets through and closes one that is idle for about 100
  seconds; the ping every 30 seconds prevents that.

{2:vercel Vercel}

Vercel serves the export as static files. Turn on clean URLs, so that
[/docs/router] serves [docs/router.html], in [vercel.json]:
[{"cleanUrls": true}]. Vercel does not read [_headers]; give the cache headers
of [/static/] in [vercel.json] instead.

Vercel does not run a container that listens on a port, so it cannot run the
server. With Docker, run the server on Fly.io or another container host, and
serve the static export from Vercel or point the domain at that host.

{1:secret The session secret}

The CSRF token of a session is a keyed hash of the session id with the secret.
The site makes a new random secret at each start, unless [SILICA_SECRET] is set:

From [site/server/main.ml]:
{@ocaml[
  let secret =
    match Sys.getenv_opt "SILICA_SECRET" with
    | Some secret when String.length secret >= 16 ->
        secret
    | Some _ ->
        prerr_endline "site: SILICA_SECRET needs at least 16 bytes";
        exit 1
    | None ->
        Silica_server_kit.Random_source.hex_id ~bytes:32
  in
]}

After a restart the sessions are gone, because the store is in memory, with or
without [SILICA_SECRET]:

- A call from a page that was open before the restart gets status 403 (the
  cookie names no session). Its machine gets [Error (Http 403)].
- A Live socket is closed with [close_session], and the page reloads once. The
  reload gets a new session and a new token (see {!page-server}).

A fixed secret matters when the sessions outlive the process or are shared by
several instances (next section): every instance must check the tokens with
the same key. Keep it out of the image and of [fly.toml]; give it as a secret
of the host, such as [fly secrets set SILICA_SECRET=...] with a value from
[openssl rand -hex 32].

{1:scale One instance}

One process holds all state that is not in the page: the session store, the
Live islands and the topic states with their subscribers. Two instances behind
a load balancer do not share them. A call or a socket that reaches the other
instance finds no session (status 403, or [close_session]), and a
{!Silica_server.Make.publish} on one instance does not reach the subscribers on
the other.

One instance goes far: on the test machine of {!page-performance}, the site
server answers about 1250 docs pages a second, and 1000 Live sessions on one
mini Linear topic cost about 120 MB of memory with 30 issues. Raise
[SILICA_MAX_CONNECTIONS] above the number of open pages with Live data, and
give the machine the memory: a topic sends its full state to each subscriber,
so memory and time grow with the state size times the subscribers.

So run one instance. To run more, an app must supply:

- A shared session store: a {!Silica_server.Session.type-ops} over a database or
  Redis, in [config.sessions].
- The same [SILICA_SECRET] on every instance.
- A shared publish: a handler publishes to a bus (Postgres [NOTIFY], Redis
  pub/sub), and each instance calls [publish] on its own server for each
  message. The [init] of a topic must read the shared data, so every instance
  starts a key at the same state.
- Islands keep their state in the instance that runs them. A load balancer
  with sticky sessions sends a socket back to that instance; without it, an
  island starts again from its first state after a reconnect.
